// Chapter 22 — Assessment of the respiratory system (on the final). The chapter's notes are Exam 2's, imported by
// tools/e3-import.js; this file adds what Exam 2 left out, checked against the chapter: one new topic, and boxes added
// to the imported topics where the detail belongs (extend).
module.exports = ({ topic, extend }) => {
  topic({
    ch: '22', title: 'The airway, lungs and aging in detail', tag: 'Foundation', after: '22-overview',
    boxes: [{
      title: 'The upper airway',
      items: [
        ['Nose and sinuses', 'the nose is the organ of **smell** (cranial nerve I receptors sit high in it). The **septum** splits it into two cavities lined with mucous membrane and a **rich blood supply**. The **turbinates** jut into the cavities and add surface area to **filter, warm and humidify** air. The **paranasal sinuses** are air-filled spaces in the facial bones, lined with ciliated membrane: they give the voice **resonance**, **lighten the skull** and **cushion** facial trauma.', ['Nose: **smell** (CN I); septum, rich blood supply', '**Turbinates** → filter, warm, humidify', '**Sinuses**: voice resonance, lighter skull, cushion trauma']],
        ['The three parts of the pharynx', 'the **nasopharynx** (behind the nose) holds the **adenoids**, which trap organisms, and the openings of the **eustachian tubes**, which equalize middle-ear pressure. The **oropharynx** (behind the mouth) carries both air and food and holds the **palatine tonsils**, part of the immune system. The **laryngopharynx** runs from the base of the tongue to the esophagus, where the airway and the food path divide.', ['**Nasopharynx**: adenoids, eustachian tubes', '**Oropharynx**: air + food; palatine tonsils', '**Laryngopharynx**: where airway and esophagus split']],
        ['The larynx and its cartilages', 'the **thyroid cartilage** is the largest (the **Adam’s apple**); the **cricoid cartilage** lies below it. The **cricothyroid membrane** joins the two **below the vocal cords**, and it is the emergency entry point for a **cricothyroidotomy**. The **glottis** is the opening between the **true vocal cords**; the **epiglottis** closes over it when you swallow. Hoarseness points to a laryngeal problem, which a specialist examines with a laryngoscope.', ['**Thyroid** cartilage = Adam’s apple; **cricoid** below', '**Cricothyroid membrane** → emergency **cricothyroidotomy**', '**Glottis** between true cords; epiglottis covers it']]
      ],
      why: 'The upper airway is built to **condition air** and **protect the lungs**: turbinates and sinuses warm and clean it, tonsils and adenoids trap organisms, and the epiglottis keeps food out. Anything that bypasses it (a tracheostomy, a tube) loses all three jobs, which is why bypassed airways need humidity and suctioning.',
      whyShort: ['Upper airway: **warm, clean, protect**', 'Bypass it → add **humidity** and **suction**']
    }, {
      title: 'The lower airway and the lungs',
      items: [
        ['Trachea and bronchi', 'the trachea lies **in front of the esophagus**. Its **6–10 C-shaped cartilage rings** are open at the back, where it shares a muscular wall with the esophagus. At the **carina** it splits into the right and left **mainstem bronchi**. The **right** bronchus is **wider, shorter and more vertical**, so an endotracheal tube passed too far, or an aspirated object, usually goes **right**. The bronchi have **complete** rings and a **ciliated, mucus-secreting** lining that sweeps particles up and out.', ['Trachea: **6–10 C-rings**, open at the back (shared wall with the esophagus)', 'Splits at the **carina**', '**Right** bronchus wider, shorter, more vertical → tubes and aspiration go **right**', 'Cilia + mucus sweep particles out']],
        ['Bronchioles, alveoli and surfactant', 'bronchioles have **no cartilage**; they stay open only because the lung’s **elastic recoil** holds them. The **acinus** is a respiratory bronchiole with its alveolar ducts and sacs. Healthy lungs hold about **290 million alveoli**, a gas-exchange surface about the size of a **tennis court**; gas exchange happens only there, because the airways are too thick. **Type II pneumocytes** make **surfactant**, which lowers surface tension; without it alveoli **collapse** (atelectasis).', ['Bronchioles: **no cartilage** → held open by **elastic recoil**', '~**290 million** alveoli ≈ a **tennis court**', 'Exchange **only in alveoli**', '**Type II pneumocytes** → surfactant; none → **atelectasis**']],
        ['The two lungs and the pleura', 'the **right lung** is larger, has **three lobes** and does about **55–60%** of the lungs’ work, so a right-lung problem costs more gas exchange than the same problem on the left. The **left lung** has **two lobes**. Each **apex** rises above the clavicle; each **base** rests on the diaphragm. The **parietal pleura** lines the chest wall and the top of the diaphragm, the **visceral pleura** covers the lung, and a thin film of fluid lets them glide.', ['**Right**: 3 lobes, **55–60%** of function', '**Left**: 2 lobes', 'Apex above the clavicle; base on the diaphragm', '**Parietal** (chest wall) + **visceral** (lung) pleura, fluid between']],
        ['Two blood supplies', 'the **bronchial** circulation is part of the systemic circulation: it **feeds the lung tissue** and takes no part in gas exchange. The **pulmonary** circulation carries oxygen-poor blood from the **right ventricle** through the pulmonary artery into capillaries wrapped around the alveoli, then back through the **pulmonary veins** to the **left atrium**. Breathing itself comes from changes in chest size and pressure: the diaphragm and chest muscles do the work, and the **back and abdominal muscles** join in when the work of breathing rises.', ['**Bronchial**: feeds lung tissue, no exchange', '**Pulmonary**: RV → alveolar capillaries → pulmonary veins → LA', 'Hard breathing recruits **back and abdominal** muscles']]
      ],
      why: 'Two details explain common exam answers. Small airways without cartilage collapse when recoil is lost (emphysema traps air), and the **vertical right bronchus** is why breath sounds are checked on **both sides** after intubation and why aspiration pneumonia favors the **right lower lobe**.',
      whyShort: ['No cartilage → airways **collapse** without recoil', '**Right** bronchus → check both sides after intubation; aspiration → **right lower lobe**']
    }, {
      title: 'Aging and the lungs (Older Adult Health box)',
      items: [
        ['What changes', 'the alveolar surface and **diffusion capacity** shrink; **elastic recoil** falls, so airways **close early** and the bronchioles and ducts widen; **residual volume rises** and **vital capacity falls**. The **cough weakens**, the **cilia** work less well, **IgA** falls and alveolar macrophages change. The diaphragm and intercostals lose strength; the vocal cords slacken and the larynx loses elasticity. **Pulmonary vascular resistance** rises and capillary blood volume falls. The chest grows rounder (**AP diameter up**), the thorax shortens, **kyphoscoliosis** progresses, the chest wall stiffens, and **osteoporosis** can deform it. The body also responds **less** to low oxygen and high CO₂.', ['↓ diffusion, ↓ **recoil** → airways close early', '**RV ↑, VC ↓**', '↓ **cough**, cilia, **IgA**; macrophages altered', 'Weaker diaphragm; slack vocal cords', '↑ pulmonary vascular resistance', '**AP ↑**, kyphoscoliosis, stiff chest wall, osteoporosis', '**Blunted** response to hypoxia and hypercarbia']],
        ['What the nurse does', 'push **pulmonary hygiene** (turn, cough, deep breathe, **incentive spirometry**), above all after surgery or in bed. Keep the patient **upright** to limit V/Q mismatch. Use all four exam techniques to tell aging from disease; **Cheyne-Stokes breathing** can occur in older adults with no disease. Encourage **oral hygiene** to clear secretions. Talk **face to face**: the voice may be soft. Check **level of consciousness and cognition**, because **confusion** may be the sign of hypoxia, and look for **subtle** hypoxia. Explain normal changes to ease worry; plan **rest periods** in exercise; encourage **calcium** to protect the chest wall bones; help the patient stay fit.', ['**Turn, cough, deep breathe**, incentive spirometer', '**Upright** → less V/Q mismatch', 'Cheyne-Stokes can be **normal** in older adults', '**Oral hygiene**; talk **face to face**', 'Check **LOC and cognition**: confusion = hypoxia?', 'Rest periods; **calcium**; stay fit']]
      ],
      why: 'An older adult has **less reserve** and **weaker alarms**: a blunted response to hypoxia, a weak cough and few symptoms. So the first sign of trouble is often **new confusion**, not breathlessness, and prevention (pulmonary hygiene, upright posture) does more than rescue.',
      whyShort: ['Less reserve, **weaker alarms**', 'First sign is often **confusion**', '**Prevention** beats rescue']
    }]
  });

  extend({
    id: '22-inhalation-injury-and-smoking-cessation',
    boxes: [{
      title: 'Smoking, vaping and quitting in detail (Boxes 22.1 and 22.2)',
      items: [
        ['Who still smokes', 'about **13%** of adults in the United States and Canada. Smoking is **more common among veterans**, especially those deployed overseas: ask every veteran and point them to the Veterans Affairs cessation services. The Joint Commission (a **National Patient Safety Goal**) requires **documenting tobacco screening** and **offering treatment**. A social smoker smokes only with others, borrows cigarettes, prefers nonsmokers’ company and doesn’t smoke for stress, so ask the direct question.', ['~**13%** of adults smoke', '**Veterans**: higher rates → VA help', 'TJC: **document screening**, **offer treatment**', 'Social smokers: ask directly']],
        ['Vaping in detail', 'electronic nicotine delivery systems include **e-cigarettes** and larger **vape pens** (often worn on a neck cord). Their **lithium batteries** have caused **burns and explosions**. The vapor carries nicotine plus toxins from the **flavorings**, linked to **bronchiolitis obliterans (“popcorn lung”)**. By February 2020 there were **2807** hospitalized cases of **EVALI** and **68 deaths**, with the highest risk from **THC** products; sales are now limited to people **21 and older**. Ask which device, which solution and which substances the patient vapes.', ['Battery **burns and explosions**', 'Flavorings → **popcorn lung**', 'EVALI: **2807** cases, **68** deaths (2020); **THC** = highest risk', 'Sales **21+**; ask device, solution, substances']],
        ['The 5 As in practice (Box 22.1)', '**Ask** and document tobacco use for **every patient at every visit**. **Advise** in a clear, strong, personal way. **Assess** whether the person is willing to try **now**. **Assist**: if willing, request drug therapy or help get over-the-counter NRT, and refer to counseling and support groups; if not, explain the harms and the benefits of quitting, listen for interest, and **reinforce** any talk of cutting down. **Arrange** follow-up starting **within the first week after the quit date**, or raise it again at the next visit for someone not ready.', ['**Every patient, every visit**', 'Willing → drugs/NRT + counseling', 'Not ready → harms, benefits, **reinforce cutting down**', 'Follow-up **within a week** of the quit date']],
        ['Quitting tips to teach (Box 22.2)', 'write down your **reasons**; set a **quit date** and keep it (taper or stop at once); find **support** (a quit partner, programs, counselors); ask about **NRT** or other drugs; remove **ashtrays and lighters**; avoid **triggers** (leave the table right after meals); keep your **hands busy**; take **five deep breaths** when an urge hits; keep **low-calorie snacks** and sugarless gum handy; drink at least **8 glasses of water** a day; start **exercise** with approval; reward yourself with the **money saved**. A slip isn’t failure: work out what triggered it and start fresh the next day.', ['**Reasons** list; **quit date**', '**Support**; ask about **NRT**', 'Remove ashtrays; avoid **triggers**', 'Busy hands; **five deep breaths**', 'Snacks, sugarless gum, **8 glasses** of water, exercise', 'A slip → find the trigger, start again']]
      ],
      why: 'Quitting is a **process with relapses**, so the plan expects them: brief advice at every visit, follow-up in the first week (when relapse is most likely), and coping skills for urges. That is why “not ready today” still gets advice and a follow-up.',
      whyShort: ['Quitting = a **process** with relapses', 'Follow up in **week one**', '“Not ready” still gets **advice**']
    }, {
      title: 'Exposures in detail (Box 22.3)',
      items: [
        ['I PREPARE, letter by letter', '**Investigate** every current and past exposure. **Present work**: dusts, fumes or chemicals, and whether breathing is **better away from work**. **Residence**: the heat source (gas, wood stove, fireplace, kerosene), whether **others at home** have the same symptoms, recent **construction** (especially in an older home), **mold** or dampness. **Environment**: air pollution, and nearby **industry, farms, airports or landfills**. **Past work**: exposure can take **years** to harm the lungs. **Activities**: hobbies such as painting, ceramics, model airplanes, refinishing furniture or woodworking. **Resources and referrals**. **Educate** at the patient’s literacy level, using sources such as the CDC, EPA, OSHA and NIOSH.', ['**I**nvestigate', '**P**resent work: better away from work?', '**R**esidence: heat, others ill, construction, **mold**', '**E**nvironment: industry, farms, airports, landfills', '**P**ast work: damage takes **years**', '**A**ctivities: paint, ceramics, refinishing', '**R**esources · **E**ducate']],
        ['Higher-risk jobs and protection', 'beyond those in the screening card: **cotton handlers**, **plastic and rubber** manufacturers and **farm workers**. On **poor air-quality days**, people should stay **indoors with the windows closed** and skip heavy exercise. Anyone exposed at work or home should wear a **protective mask** and make sure the area is **well ventilated**.', ['Also: cotton, plastic/rubber, farm workers', 'Bad air days: **indoors**, windows closed, no heavy exercise', 'Exposure: **mask** + **ventilation**']],
        ['Women and smoke', 'women have **more reactive bronchi** and **larger airways** than men, and smoke increases that reactivity, so lung function can **decline faster** in women who smoke or smoked. Check gas exchange with **pulse oximetry** in every patient.', ['Women: **more reactive** bronchi', 'Smoke → **faster decline**', 'Pulse oximetry for everyone']]
      ],
      why: 'Lung disease from exposure can appear **years** after the work ended, and it often clusters in a household or a trade. Asking about past jobs, the home and other people with the same symptoms finds the cause that a single visit’s exam can’t.',
      whyShort: ['Damage shows up **years** later', 'Clusters in **homes and trades**']
    }]
  });

  extend({
    id: '22-questions-to-ask-this-patient',
    boxes: [{
      title: 'The respiratory history (Box 22.4)',
      items: [
        ['What the history covers', 'smoking (in **pack-years**). **Childhood** illnesses: asthma, pneumonia, communicable diseases, hay fever, allergies, eczema, frequent colds, **croup**, **cystic fibrosis**. **Adult** illnesses: COVID-19, pneumonia, sinusitis, **TB**, HIV, emphysema, **sarcoidosis**, diabetes, hypertension, heart disease. **Vaccines**: influenza, pneumococcal, COVID-19 and **BCG**. Surgery or injury of the airway, hospitalizations, the date and result of the last **chest x-ray, PFT and TB test**, **weight loss, night sweats** and sleep problems, family lung disease, recent **travel**, and work and hobbies.', ['Smoking in **pack-years**', 'Childhood: asthma, croup, **CF**, allergies…', 'Adult: **TB**, HIV, sarcoidosis, heart disease…', 'Vaccines incl. **BCG**', 'Last CXR, **PFT, TB test**', '**Weight loss, night sweats**, sleep', 'Family, travel, work, hobbies']],
        ['Drugs, allergies, travel and family', 'ask about over-the-counter **cough syrups, antihistamines, decongestants and inhalants**, complementary therapies and **past** drugs: **bleomycin** and **amiodarone** can scar the lungs for good, and inhaled **marijuana** and **cocaine** reduce lung function. Ask how each allergy shows itself (wheeze, cough, sneezing, rhinitis) and how it was treated, and **document each allergen and the reaction prominently** (Action Alert). Geography matters: **histoplasmosis** (central United States and Canada), **coccidioidomycosis** and **hantavirus** (western and southwestern United States, Mexico, Central America); ask veterans where they were deployed in the past year. Family history finds inherited disease (**cystic fibrosis, emphysema**), allergy in asthma, and shared **TB** exposure.', ['OTC cough, cold and allergy drugs; **past** drugs (bleomycin, amiodarone)', 'Marijuana, cocaine ↓ lung function', '**Document** allergen + reaction prominently', 'Histo: central US/Canada; cocci + hantavirus: southwest', 'Family: **CF, emphysema**, allergy, **TB**']]
      ],
      why: 'The history narrows the cause before the stethoscope does: a drug can explain a cough or a scarred lung, a region can explain a fungal pneumonia, and a family can explain an inherited disease or a shared TB exposure.',
      whyShort: ['Drugs, places and families **explain** lung disease']
    }, {
      title: 'Cough, sputum, chest pain and dyspnea (Table 22.1)',
      items: [
        ['Analyzing the symptom', 'take the story in order: **onset**, how long it lasts, **where**, how often, whether it is **getting worse**, what comes with it, what **helps** or worsens it, and what has been tried. For a **cough**: how long, the **time of day** (on waking?) or link to activity, and whether it is productive or **dry, tickling or hacking**.', ['Onset, duration, location, frequency, **worsening**', 'What helps, what worsens, what was tried', 'Cough: time of day; **productive** or dry']],
        ['What the sputum says', 'note color, **consistency** (thin, thick, watery, frothy), odor and amount (**teaspoon, tablespoon, cup**); the lungs normally make up to about **90 mL** a day. **Mucoid**: a smoker with chronic bronchitis. **Pink and frothy**: pulmonary edema. **Rust-colored**: bacterial pneumonia. **Foul-smelling**: lung abscess. **Blood-streaked** (hemoptysis): chronic bronchitis or lung cancer. **Grossly bloody**: TB, pulmonary infarction, lung cancer or abscess.', ['Up to **90 mL/day** is normal', '**Mucoid** → smoker’s bronchitis', '**Pink frothy** → pulmonary edema', '**Rust** → bacterial pneumonia', '**Foul** → abscess', '**Bloody** → TB, infarction, cancer, abscess']],
        ['Chest pain: heart or lung?', '**cardiac** pain is usually intense and “**crushing**” and may spread to the arm, shoulder or neck. **Pulmonary** pain varies but often feels like something **rubbing** inside; it may come only on a **deep breath** or at the **end of inspiration and expiration**, and it is **not worse when you press** on the chest. Ask whether coughing, deep breathing or swallowing makes it worse.', ['Cardiac: **crushing**, radiates', 'Lung: **rubbing**, with deep breaths', 'Not worse with **pressure** on the chest']],
        ['Measuring dyspnea', 'dyspnea is **subjective**, and many patients can’t answer “are you short of breath?” A **visual analog scale** (a line from no breathlessness to the worst possible) tracks change from the patient’s **baseline**. Ask about onset (sudden or slow), duration, what relieves it (position, drugs, rest) and whether **wheeze or stridor** comes with it, then tie it to ADLs (one block? one flight?). **Orthopnea** is breathlessness lying down, relieved by sitting up. **Paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea** wakes the patient from sleep unable to breathe and is eased by sitting up; it occurs in chronic lung disease and **left-sided heart failure**.', ['**Visual analog scale** → change from baseline', 'Onset, relief, **wheeze or stridor**', 'Tie it to **ADLs**', '**Orthopnea**: lying down', '**PND**: wakes from sleep → lung disease, **left HF**']],
        ['Dyspnea classes (Table 22.1)', '**Class I**: breathless only on **more than normal** exertion; employable. **Class II**: independent in essential ADLs; breathless on **stairs or inclines** but not level walking. **Class III**: breathless during usual activities such as **showering or dressing** but manages alone; not breathless at rest; can walk a block at their own pace but can’t keep up with peers. **Class IV**: needs **help with some essential ADLs** (dressing, bathing); breathless with minimal effort, pausing on one flight, after **100 yards** or while dressing; often housebound if living alone. **Class V**: **breathless at rest**, housebound and often limited to bed or chair, dependent for most needs. The ADL key runs from **4** (normal) to **0** (too difficult to attempt).', ['**I**: only heavy exertion', '**II**: stairs, inclines', '**III**: showering, dressing — still alone', '**IV**: needs help; pauses at 100 yards', '**V**: **at rest**; dependent', 'ADL key **4 → 0**']],
        ['Feelings and coping', 'breathlessness causes **anxiety**, from **cerebral hypoxia** or the frightening sense of not getting enough air, and the thought of a diagnosis such as cancer adds fear. Chronic lung disease can change **family roles**, isolate the patient and bring money problems or job loss. Ask how the patient copes (dependence on family, withdrawal, skipping treatment), find their strengths and supports, and let them **voice their fears**.', ['Anxiety: **hypoxia** or air hunger', 'Chronic disease: roles, isolation, money, work', 'Assess **coping**; let them voice fears']]
      ],
      why: 'Dyspnea has no lab value. The classes, the analog scale and questions about blocks and stairs turn a feeling into a number you can **compare over time**, and a **change from baseline** is what signals danger.',
      whyShort: ['Turn dyspnea into a **number**', 'A **change from baseline** signals danger']
    }]
  });

  extend({
    id: '22-breath-sounds-sound-to-mechanism',
    boxes: [{
      title: 'Examining the nose, throat, neck and chest',
      items: [
        ['The nose', 'inspect the outside for deformity and the nares for symmetry, then tilt the head back for a **penlight** look inside: color, swelling, drainage, bleeding, **polyps**. The lining is normally **redder** than the mouth’s; in **allergic rhinitis** it is **pale, swollen and bluish-gray**. A **deviated septum** looks like an S; if light shines through the septum it is **perforated**, as can happen with **cocaine** use. Polyps are **pale, shiny lumps** on the turbinates. Block one naris at a time to test airflow.', ['Penlight, head tilted back', 'Allergic rhinitis: **pale, boggy, bluish-gray**', 'Light through the septum → **perforation** (cocaine)', 'Polyps: **pale, shiny** lumps', 'Block **one naris** at a time']],
        ['Throat and neck', 'press the tongue down **one side at a time** (to avoid the gag reflex) and have the patient say “**ah**”: the soft palate should rise; look at color and symmetry, drainage, swelling or ulcers, and the **tonsils**. Inspect the neck for symmetry, masses, swelling, bruises and **accessory muscle** use; feel the lymph nodes (size, shape, mobility, consistency, tenderness).', ['Tongue blade **one side at a time**', '“Ah” → soft palate rises', 'Neck: masses, **accessory muscles**, lymph nodes']],
        ['Where the trachea sits', 'it should be **midline**. It is **pushed away** from the problem by a **tension pneumothorax**, a large **pleural effusion**, a **mediastinal mass** or a **neck tumor**, and **pulled toward** it after a **pneumonectomy** or by **fibrosis** or **atelectasis**.', ['Pushed **away**: tension pneumothorax, effusion, mass, tumor', 'Pulled **toward**: pneumonectomy, fibrosis, atelectasis']],
        ['Inspecting the chest', 'sit the patient up, undressed to the waist; work from **apex to base, side to side**, looking for scars, lesions, masses and spinal curves, the rate, depth and rhythm, **equal expansion**, pursed-lip or diaphragmatic breathing and accessory muscles. The **AP-to-lateral ratio** is normally about **1:1.5**; it nears **1:1** in emphysema (**barrel chest**). Ribs normally slope down; with **air trapping** they run more **horizontally** and the intercostal spaces (normally about **2 cm**, one fingerbreadth) widen. **Retractions**, skin pulled in between the ribs or at the sternal notch on inspiration, never occur in healthy breathing: the patient is working to breathe around an **obstruction**.', ['Apex → base, **side to side**', 'AP:lateral **1:1.5**; **1:1** = barrel chest', 'Air trapping: **horizontal ribs**, wide spaces (normal ~2 cm)', '**Retractions** = breathing around an obstruction']],
        ['Palpating the chest', 'for **expansion**, put your thumbs on the spine at the level of the **ninth ribs** with your fingers around the sides: they should move apart **evenly**. Unequal expansion comes from pain, trauma or air in the pleural space, and a **lag** on one side means a lung problem. **Crepitus** (subcutaneous emphysema) feels like **crackling** under the fingers; document it and **report** it near a wound or tracheostomy or when a pneumothorax is suspected. **Fremitus** is the vibration felt as the patient speaks: **decreased** when air (pneumothorax) or fluid (effusion) fills the pleural space or a bronchus is blocked, **increased** over **pneumonia or an abscess**, because denser tissue carries vibration better.', ['Thumbs at the **9th ribs**: move apart evenly', 'Unequal: pain, trauma, pleural air; **lag** = lung problem', '**Crepitus** → report near wound, trach, pneumothorax', 'Fremitus **↓** air/fluid/blocked bronchus; **↑** pneumonia, abscess']]
      ],
      why: 'Every chest finding is about **density**. Air carries sound and vibration poorly; solid or fluid-filled lung carries them well. Consolidation therefore gives **more** fremitus, a **dull** note and **bronchial** sounds where vesicular ones belong, while air or fluid in the pleural space **separates** the lung from your hand and gives **less** of everything.',
      whyShort: ['It’s all **density**', 'Consolidation: ↑ fremitus, **dull**, bronchial sounds', 'Pleural air/fluid: **less** of everything']
    }, {
      title: 'Percussion and breath sounds (Tables 22.2–22.4)',
      items: [
        ['The five percussion notes (Table 22.2)', '**Resonance** (low, hollow, long): normal lung. **Hyperresonance** (higher, very loud, booming): **trapped air**, as in emphysema or asthma, and sometimes a pneumothorax. **Flatness** (high, soft, short, extreme dullness): normally over the **sternum**; over the lung, a **massive pleural effusion**. **Dullness** (medium, thudlike): normally over the liver and kidneys; over the lung, **atelectasis or consolidation**. **Tympany** (high, loud, drumlike): an air-filled cheek or bowel; over the lung, a **large pneumothorax**.', ['**Resonance** = normal lung', '**Hyperresonance** = trapped air', '**Flat** = sternum; massive effusion', '**Dull** = liver; atelectasis, consolidation', '**Tympany** = large pneumothorax']],
        ['Normal breath sounds (Table 22.3)', '**Bronchial** (tracheal): high-pitched, loud, harsh and hollow, with **expiration longer** than inspiration, heard over the **trachea and larynx**. **Bronchovesicular**: moderate pitch, **inspiration equal to expiration**, over the major bronchi (between the scapulae, and beside the upper sternum in the first and second intercostal spaces). **Vesicular**: low, soft, rustling like **wind in the trees**, with **inspiration longer**, over the peripheral lung. Bronchial sounds heard at the **lung edges** are abnormal: dense tissue (atelectasis, tumor, pneumonia) is carrying them. Listen with the stethoscope **firm on skin**, the patient breathing slowly and deeply through an **open mouth**, side to side through a full cycle, avoiding bone.', ['**Bronchial**: trachea; expiration longer', '**Bronchovesicular**: major bronchi; equal', '**Vesicular**: periphery; inspiration longer', 'Bronchial at the edges → **dense tissue**', 'Skin, open mouth, side to side, avoid bone']],
        ['What each added sound goes with (Table 22.4)', '**Fine crackles** (high-pitched, “**Velcro**” late in inspiration; restrictive disease): asbestosis, atelectasis, interstitial fibrosis, bronchitis, pneumonia, chronic lung disease. **Coarse crackles** (low, rattling, **change with coughing or suctioning**): bronchitis, pneumonia, tumors, **pulmonary edema**. **Wheezes** (musical, continuous, small airways, sometimes heard without a stethoscope, **don’t clear with cough**): inflammation, bronchospasm, edema, secretions, engorged vessels. **Rhonchi** (low, snoring, large airways): thick secretions, a **foreign body**, tumors. **Pleural friction rub** (loud, grating, in the **lateral** fields, painful on deep breaths): pleurisy, TB, pulmonary infarction, pneumonia, lung cancer. Document **exactly** what you hear.', ['Fine crackles: **Velcro**; fibrosis, atelectasis', 'Coarse: **change with cough**; edema, pneumonia', 'Wheeze: small airways; **don’t clear**', 'Rhonchi: large airways; secretions, foreign body', 'Rub: **lateral**, painful; pleurisy, infarction', 'Chart **exactly** what you hear']],
        ['Voice sounds and other clues', 'spoken words sound **muffled** through air-filled lung and **louder and clearer** through solid or fluid-filled tissue: the book lists **pneumonia, atelectasis, pleural effusion, tumor and abscess** as causes of louder voice sounds. (A slip for effusion: the chapter’s own fremitus section says fluid in the pleural space **dampens** transmission, and assessment texts teach that an effusion **muffles** voice sounds too, except in a band just above the fluid.) **Pallor, ash-gray skin or cyanosis** in the nail beds and mouth show a gas-exchange problem **now**; **clubbing**, weight loss, thin limbs and big neck and chest muscles show a **long-term** one. Check **endurance**: breathless at rest, or after **10–20 steps**? How often does the patient stop for breath while talking?', ['Voice sounds **louder** through dense lung', 'Cyanosis, ash-gray = **now**; clubbing, wasting = **long-term**', 'Breathless after **10–20 steps**? Pauses between words?']]
      ],
      why: 'Percussion and auscultation answer the same question as fremitus: is the lung full of **air** (resonant, vesicular), **too much air** (hyperresonant, quiet) or **solid or fluid** (dull, bronchial)? Learn the five notes and three normal sounds as a density scale and the abnormal findings sort themselves.',
      whyShort: ['Air → **resonant**, vesicular', 'Too much air → **hyperresonant**', 'Solid/fluid → **dull**, bronchial']
    }]
  });

  extend({
    id: '22-diagnostic-tests-and-your-job-around-eac',
    boxes: [{
      title: 'Labs and imaging (Table 22.5)',
      items: [
        ['Red cells, hemoglobin and hematocrit', 'RBCs **4.2–5.4** million/µL in women, **4.7–6.1** in men; hemoglobin **12–16 g/dL** (women) and **14–18 g/dL** (men); hematocrit **36–47%** (women) and **40–52%** (men). **High** values (**polycythemia**) come from extra erythropoietin in **chronic hypoxia** (COPD) or **high altitude**. **Low** values mean anemia, hemorrhage or hemolysis: less hemoglobin carries less oxygen and can cause hypoxemia.', ['RBC F **4.2–5.4**, M **4.7–6.1** million/µL', 'Hgb F **12–16**, M **14–18** g/dL', 'Hct F **36–47%**, M **40–52%**', '↑ **polycythemia**: chronic hypoxia, altitude', '↓ anemia, bleeding, hemolysis']],
        ['White cells and the differential', 'WBC **5000–10,000/mm³**: **up** with acute infection or inflammation; **down** with overwhelming infection, autoimmune disease or immunosuppressants. **Neutrophils** (55–70%): up in **bacterial** infection (pneumonia), COPD and smoking-related inflammation; down in **viral** illness (influenza). **Eosinophils** (1–4%): up in **asthma, allergies** and COPD. **Basophils** (0.5–1%): up in chronic **sinusitis** and hypersensitivity. **Lymphocytes** (20–40%): up in **viral** infection, pertussis and mononucleosis; down on **corticosteroids**. **Monocytes** (2–8%): up in **active TB**.', ['WBC **5000–10,000**', 'Neutrophils ↑ **bacterial**; ↓ viral', 'Eosinophils ↑ **asthma, allergy**', 'Basophils ↑ sinusitis, hypersensitivity', 'Lymphocytes ↑ viral; ↓ **steroids**', 'Monocytes ↑ **active TB**']],
        ['Blood gases in the table', 'PaO₂ **80–100 mm Hg** (may be lower in older adults; high from **too much oxygen**); PaCO₂ **35–45** (high in COPD, asthma, pneumonia, anesthesia or **opioids**, which is respiratory acidosis; low in hyperventilation); pH **7.35–7.45**; HCO₃⁻ **21–28 mEq/L**; SpO₂ **95–100%**. Reading them is in chapter 14.', ['PaO₂ **80–100**', 'PaCO₂ **35–45**: ↑ COPD, opioids', 'pH **7.35–7.45**; HCO₃⁻ **21–28**', 'SpO₂ **95–100%**']],
        ['Sputum tests', 'a **culture and sensitivity** names the bacteria and the antibiotics that will work; **cytology** finds **cancer cells** and can point to allergic disease.', ['**C&S**: organism + right antibiotic', '**Cytology**: cancer cells, allergy']],
        ['Imaging', 'chest x-rays are taken **back-to-front (PA) and left lateral**, and they also give a **baseline** for later films. They show pneumonia, atelectasis, pneumothorax, tumors, pleural fluid and the position of tubes and catheters, but they can look **normal** in severe chronic bronchitis, asthma or emphysema. **Sinus and facial films** show fluid levels in sinusitis. **CT** gives cross-sections of the chest to identify a lesion or a **clot**, often with IV **contrast**: ask about earlier reactions and **kidney function** (contrast can harm the kidneys). A **shellfish allergy is not a reason** to withhold contrast, because the allergy is to a muscle protein (tropomyosin), not iodine. **Metformin** may be stopped **24 hours before** contrast and restarted only after kidney function is confirmed.', ['CXR: **PA + left lateral**; baseline', 'Can look **normal** in severe COPD or asthma', 'Sinus films: fluid levels', 'CT contrast: ask about **kidneys**', '**Shellfish allergy ≠ contrast risk**', '**Metformin**: stop 24 h before; restart when kidneys OK']]
      ],
      why: 'The blood count reflects the lungs in two ways: **chronic hypoxia** drives the marrow to make more red cells (polycythemia), and the **type of white cell** that rises hints at the cause (neutrophils for bacteria, lymphocytes for viruses, eosinophils for allergy and asthma, monocytes for TB).',
      whyShort: ['Chronic hypoxia → **polycythemia**', 'Which WBC rises → **which cause**']
    }]
  });

  extend({
    id: '22-reading-the-numbers-oximetry-capnography',
    boxes: [{
      title: 'Oximetry, PFT and exercise-test details (Table 22.6)',
      items: [
        ['More about oximetry', 'the sensor goes on a **finger, toe, nose, earlobe or forehead**; record the reading as **SpO₂** so it isn’t confused with the PaO₂. Oximetry picks up desaturation **before** you can see it (dusky skin, pale or blue nail beds). Readings are also falsely low with **ambient light** (sunlight, infrared lamps), **low hemoglobin** and **edema**. Some inexpensive portable oximeters are **unreliable** in acutely ill patients. Values below **80%** may already be life-threatening, and oximeters are **least accurate at low values**, so the true saturation may be **lower** than shown.', ['Finger, toe, nose, earlobe, **forehead**', 'Record as **SpO₂**', 'Detects desaturation **before** it shows', 'Also misled by **light, low Hgb, edema**', 'Least accurate when **low**: true value may be lower']],
        ['What else PFTs do', 'PFTs measure volumes, flow rates, **diffusion**, gas exchange, airway resistance and the spread of ventilation. They **screen** before symptoms start, **track** whether a treatment should continue, change or stop, and flag **surgical patients** at risk for lung complications. A respiratory therapist runs them at the bedside or in the lab; the patient breathes through the **mouth only**, with a **nose clip**. The anxious patient fears breathlessness, so explain what will happen.', ['Screen, **track therapy**, predict **post-op** risk', 'Mouth only, **nose clip**', 'Explain to ease anxiety']],
        ['The extra values (Table 22.6)', '**FVC** reflects respiratory **muscle strength and reserve**; it falls in both obstructive and restrictive disease. **FEV₁** depends on effort and falls normally with age. **FEF 25–75%**, the flow over the middle half of the FVC, is the **most sensitive sign of small-airway obstruction**. **FRC** (air left after a normal breath out) rises with air trapping and is normal or low in restrictive disease. **DLCO**: the patient inhales a little carbon monoxide, holds it **10 seconds** and breathes out; it **falls** when the membrane is lost (emphysema, pulmonary hypertension, fibrosis) and **rises** with exercise, **polycythemia** and heart disease.', ['**FVC**: muscle strength; ↓ both types', '**FEV₁** falls with age', '**FEF 25–75%** = small-airway obstruction', '**FRC** ↑ air trapping', '**DLCO**: hold CO 10 s; ↑ polycythemia, exercise']],
        ['Exercise testing', 'on a **treadmill**, a **bicycle** or a self-paced **12-minute walk**, it shows how much work and how many ADLs the patient can manage, **why** exercise is limited and whether **oxygen is needed during exercise**. Healthy people are limited by their **circulation**; people with lung disease by their **breathing capacity or gas exchange**. Reassure the patient that trained staff watch them throughout.', ['Treadmill, bicycle or **12-minute walk**', 'Why exercise is limited; **oxygen with exercise?**', 'Healthy: circulation limits; lung disease: **breathing** limits']]
      ],
      why: 'Each value isolates one part of the system: FEV₁ and FEF 25–75% measure **airway flow**, TLC and FRC measure **trapped or lost volume**, and DLCO measures the **membrane**. Matching the abnormal value to the part tells obstruction, restriction and membrane loss apart.',
      whyShort: ['FEV₁, FEF → **airways**', 'TLC, FRC → **volume**', 'DLCO → **membrane**']
    }]
  });

  extend({
    id: '22-endoscopy-thoracentesis-and-biopsy-the-n',
    boxes: [{
      title: 'Procedure details',
      items: [
        ['Rigid or flexible bronchoscopy', '**rigid** bronchoscopy usually needs **general anesthesia** in the OR; **flexible** bronchoscopy is done in the ICU or an endoscopy suite with **low-dose sedation**. It is used to inspect the airway, place or change an **endotracheal tube**, collect **specimens**, diagnose **infections**, **stage lung cancer**, remove **secretions** that suctioning can’t clear and place **stents** to open narrowed airways. Mediastinoscopy is done in the OR under general anesthesia; its complications come mostly from the **anesthetic** and **bleeding**.', ['**Rigid**: OR, general anesthesia', '**Flexible**: ICU/suite, low-dose sedation', 'Uses: tube placement, specimens, **staging**, secretions, **stents**', 'Mediastinoscopy risks: anesthesia, **bleeding**']],
        ['During bronchoscopy', 'the provider doing it explains the outcomes, **risks and benefits**; the nurse confirms consent and **documents allergies**. A **benzodiazepine** may be given for sedation and **amnesia**, sometimes with an opioid. **Lidocaine spray** numbs the throat, and the scope passes through the **nose or mouth**. Keep **IV access**, give **oxygen** and monitor pulse, BP, respirations and SpO₂ **continuously**. Afterward, watch for **bleeding, infection and hypoxemia**.', ['Provider explains risks; nurse checks **consent, allergies**', '**Benzodiazepine** → sedation + amnesia', '**Lidocaine spray**; scope via nose or mouth', 'IV, **oxygen**, continuous monitoring', 'After: **bleeding, infection, hypoxemia**']],
        ['Thoracentesis: why and how to prepare', 'pleural fluid is examined under the microscope or drained to relieve **lung and vessel compression** and distress from cancer, **empyema**, pleurisy or TB; drugs can also be instilled. Tell the patient to expect **stinging** from the local anesthetic and **pressure** as the needle enters, and not to move, cough or breathe deeply. Ask about **allergy to local anesthetics**, confirm **consent**, expose the chest or back and **clip** hair if needed; the site depends on where the fluid is. Support the patient with pillows and **stand in front** of them so the table can’t move and they can’t fall.', ['Diagnose; drain to relieve compression; instill drugs', 'Expect **stinging** + **pressure**', 'Ask about **local anesthetic allergy**', 'Pillows; nurse **in front** of the patient']],
        ['Thoracentesis: during and after', 'a nurse practitioner or physician does it at the bedside, sometimes guided by **CT or ultrasound**; everyone wears **goggles and masks**. Fluid is aspirated slowly (a **vacuum bottle** for large volumes); a second, larger cutting needle takes a **biopsy** if needed. Then pressure and a **sterile dressing**; sometimes a drainage catheter is left in to a **water-seal** system instead of repeated taps. Afterward: a chest x-ray for **pneumothorax and mediastinal shift**, vital signs, breath sounds on the affected side, the **site for leakage or bleeding**, and watch for **reaccumulation**, **subcutaneous emphysema**, infection and **tension pneumothorax**. Encourage **deep breaths** to re-expand the lung.', ['CT/ultrasound guidance; **goggles and masks**', 'Vacuum bottle; biopsy needle', 'Pressure + **sterile dressing**; maybe a water-seal drain', 'CXR: **pneumothorax, mediastinal shift**', 'Watch: leak, **reaccumulation**, SC emphysema, **tension pneumothorax**', 'Encourage **deep breaths**']],
        ['Subcutaneous emphysema', 'air in the layers of the skin, seen as **swelling around the puncture site** that **crackles** when pressed. It means a **persistent air leak** from a tear in the pleura. A **small**, contained area may need no treatment, but if it spreads into the **neck**, it can threaten the **airway**.', ['Swelling that **crackles**', 'Means a **pleural air leak**', 'Small: may need nothing; **neck** → airway threat']],
        ['Lung biopsy details', 'it confirms **inflammation, cancer, infection** or other lung disease. Many patients link “biopsy” with **cancer**, so explore their feelings. **Percutaneous**: in radiology, under **CT or fluoroscopy**, usually side-lying, with a local anesthetic (they will still feel **pressure**) and a spinal-type needle, then a dressing and a **CT or chest x-ray to rule out pneumothorax**. **Open**: a **thoracotomy** in the OR with a **chest tube** to reinflate the lung. Hemoptysis afterward is usually **scant and brief**; frank bleeding is rare.', ['Biopsy = fear of **cancer**: explore feelings', 'Percutaneous: CT/fluoro, side-lying, **pressure** felt', 'Always a **CT or CXR** after', 'Open: thoracotomy + **chest tube**', 'Hemoptysis usually **scant, brief**']]
      ],
      why: 'Anything that crosses the pleura can let air in. That single fact links the x-ray after every thoracentesis and needle biopsy, the crackling skin of subcutaneous emphysema, and the warning that swelling spreading to the **neck** threatens the airway.',
      whyShort: ['Crossing the pleura can **let air in**', 'X-ray after; watch **crackling** skin', 'Spreading to the **neck** → airway']
    }]
  });

  extend({
    id: '22-oxygen-as-a-drug-devices-doses-hazards',
    boxes: [{
      title: 'Devices in detail (Tables 22.7 and 22.8)',
      items: [
        ['Choosing the device', 'the choice depends on the **concentration** needed, what each device can deliver, how **precise** it must be, comfort, **humidity** and mobility; consult the respiratory therapist with any question. A patient on a mask who can eat gets a **nasal cannula at meals**, then the mask again. Up to **50 feet** of tubing allows walking, but long tubing is a **fall risk** for an unsteady patient.', ['Concentration, precision, comfort, humidity, mobility', 'Mask + meals → **cannula at meals**', 'Up to **50 ft** tubing: a **fall risk**']],
        ['Cannula care (Table 22.7)', 'set the prongs in the nares with the openings **facing the patient**, following the curve of the nose. Apply **water-soluble jelly** to the nares as needed. Check that the nostrils are **open** (congestion or a deviated septum blocks delivery), and watch the **rate and depth** of breathing, which show how well the oxygen is getting in. The cannula is the usual device for **long-term** therapy and chronic lung disease.', ['Prongs follow the nose; openings **toward the patient**', '**Water-soluble jelly** PRN', 'Check **nostrils open**', 'Watch rate and depth', 'Device for **long-term** use']],
        ['Simple and partial rebreather masks', 'a mask must **fit snugly** (a loose one lowers the FiO₂). Care for the **skin** under the mask and straps. A mask makes it hard to clear the mouth, so watch for **aspiration** if the patient vomits, and support a patient who feels **claustrophobic**. The **partial rebreather** has a reservoir bag but **no flaps**; each breath re-inhales about **a third** of the exhaled air, which is still rich in oxygen. Keep the bag from **twisting or kinking** and set the flow to keep it inflated (the table says two-thirds full).', ['**Snug** fit; skin care', 'Vomiting → **aspiration** risk', 'Support **claustrophobia**', 'Partial rebreather: bag, **no flaps**; rebreathes ~⅓', 'No **kinked** bag; keep it inflated']],
        ['How the nonrebreather works', 'a **one-way valve** between mask and bag lets the patient draw only from the bag and stops exhaled air from going back into it; **flaps** over the exhalation ports keep room air from diluting the oxygen. It can deliver **more than 90%**, depending on the breathing pattern. Because the patient is **unstable** and may need **intubation**, assess closely whether the oxygen need is being met.', ['**One-way valve**: breathe only from the bag', '**Flaps** keep room air out', 'Can exceed **90%**', 'Unstable: may need **intubation**']],
        ['High-flow devices (Table 22.8)', '**Venturi**: **24–50%** at the flow the manufacturer sets for each adapter (usually **4–10 L/min**), by color-coded adapters or a dial; keep the **entrainment opening uncovered**, check that the flow matches the FiO₂, keep the mask snug and the tubing free of kinks, watch for **dry mucous membranes**, and switch to a cannula at meals. **Aerosol mask, face tent, tracheostomy collar and T-piece**: **24–100%** at **10 L/min or more** with high humidity. **Mist** should be visible on both inspiration and expiration; **empty the condensation** (so it can’t flow into a tracheostomy); change the water container as needed; keep the **exhalation port open**; and position a **T-piece** so it doesn’t pull on the tube. The **high-flow nasal cannula** sets FiO₂ and flow separately, causes **less skin breakdown** than a tight NPPV mask, and is titrated by **pulse oximetry**.', ['**Venturi**: 24–50%, ~4–10 L/min; opening **uncovered**', 'Aerosol, face tent, trach collar, **T-piece**: 24–100%, ≥10 L/min', 'See **mist** in and out; **drain condensation**', 'T-piece mustn’t **pull** on the tube', 'HFNC: titrate by **oximetry**; less skin breakdown']],
        ['NPPV modes and uses', 'NPPV can give **oxygen or room air** through a **nasal mask, nasal pillows or a full-face mask**. Modes: **CPAP** (one pressure throughout), **volume- or flow-limited** (a set tidal volume with each effort) and **pressure-limited** (pressure support, pressure control and **BiPAP**). CPAP helps **postoperative atelectasis**, **cardiac pulmonary edema** and COPD. CPAP and BiPAP are used **after extubation** to prevent reintubation, though a **high-flow cannula** is an alternative that lets the patient talk and clear secretions. NPPV also eases dyspnea in **palliative care**, including for patients who have asked **not to be intubated**. Patients who use CPAP or BiPAP at home for **sleep apnea** often bring their own machine to the hospital and still need it there.', ['Oxygen or room air; nasal mask, pillows or full face', 'Modes: **CPAP**, volume-limited, pressure-limited (**BiPAP**)', 'After **extubation**; HFNC is an alternative', '**Palliative** use, incl. do-not-intubate', 'Home **sleep apnea** machines come along']]
      ],
      why: 'Low-flow devices **mix with room air**, so what the patient gets depends on how they breathe; high-flow devices supply the **whole breath**, so the dose is exact. That is why a Venturi mask is chosen when the FiO₂ must be precise (a CO₂ retainer) and why a nonrebreather’s valves and bag are checked: if they fail, room air or exhaled air takes the oxygen’s place.',
      whyShort: ['Low-flow: **mixes** with room air', 'High-flow: **whole breath**, exact dose', 'Precise need → **Venturi**', 'Check NRB **valves and bag**']
    }, {
      title: 'Safety routines, humidity and home oxygen (Box 22.5)',
      items: [
        ['Every shift (Box 22.5)', 'check the **order** (device and flow or percentage); humidify at **4 L/min or more**; make sure the equipment works; check the skin at the **ears, back of the neck and face every 4–8 hours**; give **mouth care every 8 hours** and check the nose and mouth for cracks; **rinse the cannula or mask** in clear warm water and clean the skin under the tubing and straps every 4–8 hours; use a **nonpetroleum** cream on the nostrils, face and lips; keep the tubing from pulling on the face or airway; allow **no smoking, candles or matches**; **document the response**; make sure there is **enough oxygen for transport**; and work with the respiratory therapist.', ['Check the **order**', 'Humidify **≥ 4 L/min**', 'Skin **q4–8h**; **mouth care q8h**', 'Rinse the cannula/mask **q4–8h**', '**Nonpetroleum** cream', 'No smoking or flames', '**Document** the response; oxygen for **transport**']],
        ['Fire and toxicity details', '**alcohol-based hand rubs are allowed** in rooms where oxygen is used; the ban is on high-alcohol or oil-based solutions. Surgical fires need an **ignition source** (cautery, lasers), a **fuel** and an **oxidizer** (oxygen or nitrous oxide). Oxygen toxicity looks like ARDS and progresses from **dyspnea, dry cough, substernal pain, GI upset and crackles** to a falling **vital capacity**, stiff lungs and **hypoxemia**, then atelectasis, pulmonary edema, hemorrhage and **hyaline membranes**. NPPV or mechanical ventilation can lower the oxygen needed, and the dose is reduced **as soon as the condition allows**.', ['**Hand rubs OK**; no oil or high-alcohol products', 'Fire = **ignition + fuel + oxidizer**', 'Toxicity: early cough, pain → ↓ VC, hypoxemia → **hyaline membranes**', 'Lower the dose **as soon as possible**']],
        ['Humidity and infection', 'besides the **bubble humidifier**, a **large-volume jet nebulizer** gives an aerosol mist, and a **heated nebulizer** is used with an **artificial airway**, which bypasses the nose that normally warms and moistens air. Use **sterile water** and an adequate flow. Condensation in the tubing is a source of **infection**: drain it by disconnecting the tubing **as briefly as possible** (the patient gets no oxygen meanwhile) or through a **water trap**, and **never back into the humidifier**. Change the humidifier when the water is **cloudy** or has particles. Change equipment per policy, from about **every 24 hours** for humidifiers to **every 7 days** for cannulas and masks.', ['Jet nebulizer = aerosol; **heated** for artificial airways', '**Sterile water**', 'Drain condensation **briefly**, never back in', 'Change when **cloudy**', 'Humidifiers ~**24 h**; cannulas/masks ~**7 days**']],
        ['Home oxygen sources', 'the patient must be **stable** first, and the Medicare criteria differ when hypoxemia isn’t from lung disease or oxygen is needed only at **night or with exercise**. **Compressed gas** comes in **green** tanks: a large **H cylinder** at home and an **E tank** (or smaller) for travel. Check the **gauge daily** and keep tanks in a **stand or rack**: a tank that falls can **decompress and shoot around** uncontrollably. **Liquid oxygen** is lightweight, carried in a thermos-like container filled from a stationary reservoir; it **lasts longer but costs more**. A **concentrator** removes nitrogen from room air to give **over 90%** oxygen and needs **power**, so teach a **backup** for outages. **Ultralight portable** concentrators charge from a car’s **DC outlet** or the wall and are **approved for airplanes**, improving adherence, mobility and quality of life. Review safety with **everyone** in the home.', ['Patient must be **stable**', '**Green** tanks: H at home, **E** for travel', '**Gauge daily**; tank in a **stand**', '**Liquid**: lasts longer, costs more', '**Concentrator**: > 90%, needs **power** → backup', 'Portable units: car DC outlet, **airplanes**']]
      ],
      why: 'Each routine guards against one of oxygen’s side effects: humidity and skin care against **drying and pressure injury**, drainage and equipment changes against **infection**, no flames against **fire**, and titrating down against **toxicity**. At home the risks are the same, plus running out: check the gauge and have a backup.',
      whyShort: ['Humidity, skin → **drying, pressure**', 'Drainage, changes → **infection**', 'No flames → **fire**', 'Titrate down → **toxicity**', 'Home: **gauge** + **backup**']
    }]
  });
};
